by X-Coder264 at 2026-09-25T09:56:44Z

Thanks for the suggestion. The purpose of `retired` is to make read permission explicit for a particular composite. The client names in a ciphertext are untrusted, so `main` should read only through clients it explicitly authorizes. When replacing `old` with `new`, it must stop creating wrappings with `old` while still reading historical ciphertexts that may have only an `old` wrapping. `members: {new: ~}` with `retired: [old]` expresses both decisions for `main`.

A per-client `encrypt: false` option could also work if `old` remained listed in `main` and `CompositeKms` learned to skip it on writes. Currently, the composite writes through every client in `members` and requires each member to support encryption, decryption, and data-key generation even on reads.

Reading a wrapped data key is different from ordinary `decrypt()`. For data keys created by the composite, the first member creates its wrapping with `generateDataKey()`, so reading that wrapping later requires `unwrapDataKey()`; some providers cannot read it with `decrypt()`. Both methods belong to `DataKeyGeneratorInterface`. A wrapper implementing only decryption interfaces would therefore be unable to read those old wrappings. Implementing `DataKeyGeneratorInterface` to retain unwrapping would also expose `generateDataKey()` to callers. A read-only wrapper would need a separate unwrapping capability, or the composite would need another way to handle read-only members.

For this fix, I prefer `retired` because it keeps read and write membership explicit per composite without changing how `old` behaves elsewhere.

